# CODEOWNERS — ownership of the CI trust boundary (issue #2910 round 4).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The `required` aggregate reads its registry, its
# aggregator and its ruby modules from the pull request's BASE ref, so a PR
# cannot rewrite the check that gates it. The acknowledged RESIDUAL is narrower:
# a merged PR can still change the classifier of a tier it was itself gated by,
# and the design named "CODEOWNERS on .github/ and scripts/ci/" as the
# complementary control for that. No CODEOWNERS file existed anywhere in the
# repo, so `require_code_owner_reviews` had nothing to resolve and the named
# control did not exist. This file makes the ownership real.
#
# WHAT IT ACTUALLY ENFORCES TODAY, stated honestly rather than assumed:
#   * GitHub auto-requests review from the owners below on any PR touching these
#     paths — real, immediate visibility on a trust-boundary edit.
#   * It does NOT block a merge. The LIVE branch protection on `main` has
#     `require_code_owner_reviews: false` and `required_approving_review_count: 0`
#     (the repo merges on the `required` check, per the autonomy doctrine in
#     CLAUDE.md). The checked-in `.github/branch-protection.json` says `true`;
#     that file has DRIFTED from the live setting and is not what GitHub applies.
#   * Turning the residual into a merge-blocking control is an owner decision
#     (it would require a human review on every agent PR touching `.github/**`),
#     not something this change makes unilaterally. See
#     openspec/changes/required-aggregates-sibling-tiers/design.md.
#
# The last matching pattern wins, so keep the trust-boundary entries last.
# scripts/tests/test_gating_registry_policy.sh asserts this file exists and
# covers both trust-boundary trees; deleting it reds the gate.

# The CI mechanism itself: workflows, actions, the gating-tier registry, and the
# branch-protection description.
/.github/ @pmcfadin

# Everything the `required` aggregate executes to decide the merge gate.
/scripts/ci/ @pmcfadin
